service is set up to scrape that. \n\nWait, the user-service\'s pod is running, but the metrics query isn\'t getting any data. So the problem might be that the service isn\'t properly set up to expose the metrics. Let me check the service\'s configuration again. The service is ClusterIP, so it\'s only accessible within the cluster. But if Prometheus is configured to scrape metrics from that service, then it should work. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the pod isn\'t actually running the metrics server. Let me check the pod\'s logs to see if there are any errors. Wait, the user has the exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd tool, so maybe I can check the logs of the user-service pod. \n\nWait, the user-service pod\'s container is named "user-service", so I can use kubectl logs to check the logs. If there are errors in the logs, that might indicate why the metrics aren\'t being scraped. For example, if the application isn\'t starting correctly, or if the metrics endpoint is not accessible. \n\nSo the next step is to check the logs of the user-service pod to see if there are any issues. That would help determine if the problem is with the application not exposing metrics, or if there\'s a configuration issue with the service or Prometheus.\n</think>\n\nexec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \n"kubectl logs user-service-559b79c875-gcl2q -n social-network"  \nThe service and pod configurations appear correct, but the metrics query returns no results. This suggests the application might not be exposing metrics on the expected port (9090) or there could be an issue with the metrics endpoint (e.g., `/metrics`). Checking the pod logs will reveal if the application is running correctly and whether metrics are being served, which is critical for diagnosing the root cause.', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 674, 'prompt_tokens': 5369, 'total_tokens': 6043, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfd33-1a6a-7763-89c1-3354dcff473f-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 5369, 'output_tokens': 674, 'total_tokens': 6043}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='0d2c8519-cb00-49e0-a2f9-71a01ebf7a5a'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to figure out why the user-service in their social network application isn\'t showing up in metrics. They\'ve already checked the deployments, pods, and service configurations. The service is set to ClusterIP with port 9090, and the pod is running with the correct labels. But when they query Prometheus for up{job="user-service"}, there are no results. \n\nHmm, the next step is to check the pod logs. The logs might show if the application is actually exposing metrics on the correct port. Maybe the service is configured correctly, but the application inside the pod isn\'t serving metrics on port 9090. Or perhaps there\'s a misconfiguration in the metrics endpoint, like the path is different. \n\nBy looking at the logs, they can verify if the service is running and if there are any errors related to metrics exposure. If the logs indicate that the metrics endpoint is not being served, that would point to a problem in the application\'s configuration. Alternatively, if the logs show the service is running but not listening on the expected port, that could be a network issue or a container setup problem.\n\nSo, using kubectl logs to check the user-service pod\'s logs makes sense here. It\'s a straightforward way to get more insight into what\'s happening inside the container. If the logs don\'t show any issues, maybe they need to check the metrics endpoint path or ensure that the Prometheus job is correctly configured to scrape from the service.\n</think>\n\n', additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl logs user-service-559b79c875-gcl2q -n social-network"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-86640dc3bfb79419', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 356, 'prompt_tokens': 6388, 'total_tokens': 6744, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfd33-3a8e-7ed1-a642-205f15f70cfe-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl logs user-service-559b79c875-gcl2q -n social-network'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-86640dc3bfb79419', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 6388, 'output_tokens': 356, 'total_tokens': 6744}), ToolMessage(content="[2026-Mar-17 19:06:13.334322] <info>: (UserHandler.h:932:GetMachineId) MAC address = 9e:d4:40:b4:b5:8f\n[2026-Mar-17 19:06:13.334642] <info>: (UserService.cpp:63:main) machine_id = e4b\n[2026-Mar-17 19:06:13.635146] <error>: (utils_mongodb.h:76:CreateIndex) Error in createIndexes: No suitable servers found: `serverSelectionTimeoutMS` expired: [unknown connection error calling ismaster on 'user-mongodb:27017']\n[2026-Mar-17 19:06:13.635582] <error>: (UserService.cpp:80:main) Failed to create mongodb index, try again\n[2026-Mar-17 19:06:14.935087] <error>: (utils_mongodb.h:76:CreateIndex) Error in createIndexes: No suitable servers found: `serverSelectionTimeoutMS` expired: [connection refused calling ismaster on 'user-mongodb:27017']\n[2026-Mar-17 19:06:14.935164] <error>: (UserService.cpp:80:main) Failed to create mongodb index, try again\n[2026-Mar-17 19:06:16.234109] <error>: (utils_mongodb.h:76:CreateIndex) Error in createIndexes: No suitable servers found: `serverSelec