ments" and saw user-service as a deployment. But the metrics query for up{job="user-service"} returned empty. So maybe the user-service isn\'t actually running. But the deployment is marked as READY. That\'s conflicting. Maybe the service isn\'t properly exposed, or the pods are not healthy. Alternatively, maybe the metrics query is incorrect. Alternatively, the service might not be properly registered with Prometheus. Alternatively, the pods might be crashing but the deployment is still showing READY because it\'s restarting. So, to check the actual pods, the next step is to get the pods in the namespace and check their status. If the pod is not running, then the deployment might have issues. Also, checking the logs of the user-service pod could help identify if there are any errors. But first, need to confirm if the pod exists. So the next tool call should be to get the pods in the namespace. Wait, the user already did that in a previous step, but maybe they didn\'t check the user-service specifically. Let me check the previous tool calls. The user first called get_services, then get_metrics for user-service, then exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd with "kubectl get deployments" and then "kubectl get pods". The response for "kubectl get pods" didn\'t show user-service. Wait, the response from the tool call "kubectl get pods -n social-network" wasn\'t provided here. Wait, the user\'s last tool call was "kubectl get deployments" and the response listed user-service as a deployment. But the metrics for user-service were empty. So maybe the user-service is not actually running. But the deployment is marked as READY. That\'s confusing. Maybe the deployment is not properly configured, or the pods are not healthy. Alternatively, the service might not be properly exposed. So the next step is to check the pods for user-service. So using kubectl get pods -n social-network to see if the user-service pod is present. If it\'s not there, then the deployment might have issues. If it is there but not running, then check the logs. So the next tool call should be exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd with "kubectl get pods -n social-network" to check the pods. Wait, the user already did that in a previous step, but maybe the response wasn\'t shown. Wait, looking back, the user\'s last tool call was "kubectl get deployments" and the response included user-service. Then the user\'s next step was to call get_metrics again. But the user hasn\'t checked the pods yet. Wait, the user\'s previous tool call was "kubectl get deployments" and the response included user-service. Then the user called get_metrics for user-service, which returned empty. So the next step is to check the pods for user-service. So the correct tool is exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd with "kubectl get pods -n social-network" to see if the user-service pod is running. If the pod is not there, then the deployment might be misconfigured. If the pod is there but not running, then check the logs. So the next tool call should be to get the pods in the namespace.\n</think>\n\nexec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \n"kubectl get pods -n social-network"  \nTo verify if the user-service pod is running, I need to check the current state of pods in the social-network namespace. The previous deployment check showed the user-service as READY, but the metrics query returned no results. This discrepancy suggests the pod might not be actively running or exposed correctly. Confirming the pod\'s existence and status is critical to validate whether the service is operational or if there\'s a deployment/health issue.', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 992, 'prompt_tokens': 2462, 'total_tokens': 3454, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfd32-4762-7da0-80a8-34726fb1b34