ed: [connection refused calling ismaster on 'user-mongodb:27017']\n[2026-Mar-17 19:06:24.029261] <error>: (UserService.cpp:80:main) Failed to create mongodb index, try again\n[2026-Mar-17 19:06:25.328144] <error>: (utils_mongodb.h:76:CreateIndex) Error in createIndexes: No suitable servers found: `serverSelectionTimeoutMS` expired: [connection refused calling ismaster on 'user-mongodb:27017']\n[2026-Mar-17 19:06:25.328267] <error>: (UserService.cpp:80:main) Failed to create mongodb index, try again\n[2026-Mar-17 19:06:27.124589] <info>: (UserService.cpp:94:main) Starting the user-service server ...\n", id='6b310eeb-1b5e-46f1-bceb-4abe9f58e5a8', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-86640dc3bfb79419'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='ecf1684d-d1ac-47bf-9048-06f47378b848'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user-service pod is running, but the metrics aren\'t showing up. The logs indicate that the service is having trouble connecting to the MongoDB instance. The error messages mention "connection refused" when trying to connect to \'user-mongodb:27017\'. \n\nFirst, I need to check if the MongoDB service is properly set up. The user-service is trying to connect to user-mongodb, so I should verify the MongoDB service\'s configuration. Maybe the service isn\'t exposed correctly, or the pods aren\'t running. Let me check the MongoDB service using kubectl.\n\nI\'ll use the exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd tool to get the details of the user-mongodb service. If the service isn\'t running or if there are no endpoints, that could explain the connection issues. Also, checking the pods for user-mongodb would help see if they\'re up and running. If the MongoDB pods are down, that\'s a problem. \n\nAnother angle: maybe the network policies are blocking the connection. But since the user-service is in the same namespace, maybe the service is misconfigured. Let me first check the service and pods for user-mongodb.\n</think>\n\nexec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \n"kubectl get service user-mongodb -n social-network"  \nThe logs indicate the user-service cannot connect to `user-mongodb:27017`. To diagnose this, I need to verify if the `user-mongodb` service is properly exposed and if its pods are running. If the service has no endpoints or is misconfigured (e.g., incorrect port or selector), the connection would fail, causing the repeated "connection refused" errors in the logs. This step will confirm the service\'s availability and configuration.', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 358, 'prompt_tokens': 6978, 'total_tokens': 7336, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfd33-4f2e-7ef2-b7b1-d09790f7ef54-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 6978, 'output_tokens': 358, 'total_tokens': 7336}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='425342b8-2e60-43bc-a15d-192a642ca83c'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user-service logs are showing errors connecting to user-mongodb:27017. The error messages mention "connection refused" and "No suitable servers found". So, the first thing I need to check is whether the user-mongodb service is up and running.\n\nI should start by checking the service configuration for user-mongodb. Using kubectl to get the service details would help. The command would be "kubectl get service user-mongodb -n social-network". This will show the service\'s endpoints, ports, and selectors. If the endpoints are <none>, that means no pods are matching the service\'s selector, which could be why the connection is failing.\n\nWait, in the previous steps, when I checked the user-service\'s endpoints, they were <none> because the selector wasn\'t matching. Maybe a similar issue is happening here with user-mongodb. Let me verify the service\'s configuration. If the service isn\'t properly pointing to the MongoDB pods, then the user-service can\'t connect. Also, checking the service\'s ports to ensure that 27017 is correctly exposed. If the service is using a different port, that could be the problem. \n\nAnother thing to consider is the MongoDB pods themselves. Even if the service is correctly configured, if the pods are not running or the MongoDB instance isn\'t accepting connections, the service won\'t have endpoints. But since the user-service is trying to connect to user-mongodb, the service must exist. However, the endpoints being empty suggests that the pods aren\'t being selected by the service. \n\nSo, the next step is to check the user-mongodb service\'s details. If the service is misconfigured, like the selector not matching the pods\' labels, that would explain the connection issues. Also, checking if the service is of type ClusterIP and if the pods are actually running. If the MongoDB pods are down or not properly labeled, the service won\'t have endpoints. \n\nI should run the command to get the service details for user-mongodb. That should give me the necessary information to determine if the service is correctly set up and if there are any issues with the endpoints or selectors.\n</think>\n\n', additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl get service user-mongodb -n social-network"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-b852ce3a255ed9e5', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 481, 'prompt_tokens': 7996, 'total_tokens': 8477, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfd33-60f0-71a3-9932-2efee47f888d-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl get service user-mongodb -n social-network'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-b852ce3a255ed9e5', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 7996, 'output_tokens': 481, 'total_tokens': 8477}), ToolMessage(content='NAME           TYPE        CLUSTER-IP       EXTERNAL-IP   PORT(S)     AGE\nuser-mongodb   ClusterIP   10.102.239.104   <none>        27017/TCP   3m31s\n', id='bec1a0d8-8af8-43b3-9e10-936187956e57', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-b852ce3a255ed9e5'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='674abe46-3c5d-479f-a736-a1270d7b3b3d'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user-service logs are showing connection issues to user-mongodb:27017. The service for user-mongodb exists and is of type ClusterIP, but the pods might not be running or the endpoints might not be correctly set up. I need to check the endpoints for the user-mongodb service to see if there are any pods associated with it. If there are no endpoints, that means the service isn\'t routing traffic to any pods, which could be due to misconfigured selectors or pods not being ready. So, the next step is to get the endpoints for the user-mongodb service. That will help determine if the service is properly connected to the MongoDB pods.\n</think>\n\nexec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \n"kubectl get endpoints user-mongodb -n social-network"  \nThe `user-mongodb` service exists, but the logs show connection failures. To confirm if the service is properly routing traffic to MongoDB pods, I need to check its endpoints. If there are no endpoints or if the endpoints are incorrect (e.g., wrong IP/ports), the service would fail to connect, causing the "connection refused" errors in the user-service logs. This will validate whether the service is correctly linked to the MongoDB pods.', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 258, 'prompt_tokens': 7246, 'total_tokens': 7504, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfd33-7b8b-7931-a2d2-379d50c45141-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 7246, 'output_tokens': 258, 'total_tokens': 7504}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='47d67051-05a4-4b5a-a5e1-793267a9dc72'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let's see. The user-service is having trouble connecting to user-mongodb. The logs show repeated connection refused errors. I checked the user-mongodb service, and it exists with the correct port. But maybe the endpoints are missing. So, I need to check the endpoints for user-mongodb. If there are no endpoints, that means the pods for MongoDB aren't being selected by the service. Let me run the command to get the endpoints.\n\nWait, the previous step was getting the service, which showed the ClusterIP. Now, getting the endpoints will tell if there are any pods associated. If the endpoints are empty, then the service isn't pointing to any pods. That would explain why user-service can't connect. So, the next tool call should be to get the endpoints of user-mongodb in the social-network namespace. That should help determine if the service is properly linked to the MongoDB pods.\n</think>\n\n", additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl get endpoints user-mongodb -n social-network"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-ae6d10802e68101a', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 222, 'prompt_tokens': 8261, 'total_tokens': 8483, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfd33-881f-7d71-8836-ef570a3722f8-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl get endpoints user-mongodb -n social-network'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-ae6d10802e68101a', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 8261, 'output_tokens': 222, 'total_tokens': 8483}), ToolMessage(content='NAME           ENDPOINTS            AGE\nuser-mongodb   10.244.1.230:27017   3m37s\n', id='a860f663-34a2-40cd-877b-c39b928e1881', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-ae6d10802e68101a'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='d163637c-844e-4d99-971e-b604fe70ad10'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user-service is having trouble connecting to user-mongodb. The logs show repeated connection refused errors. The service exists and has endpoints, so the service is correctly configured. The endpoints are pointing to 10.244.1.230:27017. Now, I need to check if the MongoDB pod is actually running and if the port is correctly exposed.\n\nWait, the user-mongodb service\'s endpoints are pointing to a pod IP. Let me check the pods for user-mongodb. If the pod is not running, the endpoints might not be correct. Also, maybe the MongoDB container isn\'t listening on port 27017. Let me check the pod\'s status and logs.\n\nSo, the next step is to get the pods for user-mongodb and check their status. If the pod is not running, that\'s a problem. Also, checking the container logs could show if MongoDB is starting correctly and if there are any issues with the port binding. Let me use kubectl to get the pods and then check the logs.\n</think>\n\nexec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \n"kubectl get pods -n social-network | grep user-mongodb"  \nTo verify if the `user-mongodb` pod is running and healthy. If the pod is not in the "Running" state or has crashes, it would explain the connection failures. This will confirm whether the MongoDB instance is operational and properly associated with the service\'s endpoints.', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': Usage(completion_tokens=306, prompt_tokens=7486, total_tokens=7792, completion_tokens_details=None, prompt_tokens_details=None), 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfd33-9577-7410-ab31-27a23644b5f3-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 7486, 'output_tokens': 306, 'total_tokens': 7792}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='7bbaf0cb-2350-4d29-add4-7ce073840780')]]
