ng, maybe the issue is with the service exposure or the metrics endpoint. The user-service might not be properly exposed as a Service in Kubernetes, so metrics can\'t be collected. Alternatively, the metrics endpoint might be misconfigured or not accessible.\n\nThe available tools include get_metrics, which was already used, and get_services, get_operations, get_dependency_graph, and others. Since the problem might be with the service\'s exposure, checking the service definition could help. Using exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd to get the service details would be useful. For example, running \'kubectl get service user-service -n social-network\' would show if the service is correctly exposed with the right ports and selectors. If the service isn\'t set up properly, that could explain why metrics aren\'t being collected even though the pod is running.\n\nAnother angle is checking the logs of the user-service pod to see if there are any errors or issues. Using \'kubectl logs user-service-559b79c875-gcl2q -n social-network\' could reveal problems like application errors, misconfigurations, or issues with the metrics server. However, the user might not have access to logs, or maybe the logs are not showing the issue. Alternatively, checking the metrics configuration in Prometheus to ensure it\'s scraping the user-service\'s metrics endpoint correctly could be necessary. But since the user has the get_metrics tool, they can query specific metrics. However, the initial query didn\'t return any data, so maybe the service isn\'t being scraped. \n\nWait, the user already checked the deployments and pods, and they are all running. The next logical step is to verify the service configuration. If the service isn\'t exposed, the metrics endpoint wouldn\'t be accessible. So, using kubectl to get the service details would be the next step. If the service is correctly exposed, then maybe the metrics endpoint is misconfigured. Alternatively, checking the endpoints to see if the pod is correctly associated with the service. \n\nSo, the best tool here is exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd with the command \'kubectl get service user-service -n social-network\' to check the service\'s configuration. If that\'s not available, maybe check the endpoints. Alternatively, if the service is correctly set up, then the issue might be with the metrics server or the Prometheus configuration. But given the tools available, checking the service configuration is the next step.\n</think>\n\nexec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \n"kubectl get service user-service -n social-network"  \nThe user-service pod is running, but the metrics query returned no results. This suggests the service might not be properly exposed or configured to expose metrics. Checking the service configuration will confirm if the service is correctly set up with the right ports, selectors, and exposure type (e.g., ClusterIP, NodePort). This will help identify if the service is unreachable or misconfigured, which could explain the missing metrics.', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 713, 'prompt_tokens': 3641, 'total_tokens': 4354, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfd32-878f-7a42-87aa-11bf60bd16ba-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 3641, 'output_tokens': 713, 'total_tokens': 4354}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='b066201d-9614-48c1-b52c-49fc690b7f08'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to diagnose an issue with the user-service in their social network application. So far, they\'ve checked the deployments and pods, and everything seems to be running. But when they queried the metrics for the user-service, there were no results. That\'s confusing because the pod is up and running.\n\nHmm, the next step is to check the service configuration for user-service. If the service isn\'t properly exposed, the metrics might not be accessible. So using kubectl to get the service details makes sense. The command would be "kubectl get service user-service -n social-network". This will show the service\'s YAML configuration, including ports, selectors, and type. \n\nWait, maybe the service is using a ClusterIP that\'s not reachable, or the ports aren\'t correctly set up. Also, if the service isn\'t exposing the metrics endpoint, Prometheus wouldn\'t pick it up. The user-service might have a metrics endpoint on a specific port, but if the service isn\'t configured to expose that port, the metrics won\'t be collected. \n\nBy checking the service details, they can verify if the port used for metrics is correctly mapped and if the selector matches the pod\'s labels. If there\'s a mismatch, that could be the root cause. Also, if the service type is ClusterIP and the pods aren\'t in the same namespace, that could be an issue. \n\nSo, running that kubectl command will provide the necessary information to troubleshoot the service exposure and configuration. Once they have the service details, they can cross-reference with the pod\'s labels and port settings to ensure everything is aligned. If the service is misconfigured, they can adjust it accordingly to fix the metrics issue.\n</think>\n\n', additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl get service user-service -n social-network"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-9bfcfd0b36886833', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 387, 'prompt_tokens': 4648, 'total_tokens': 5035, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfd32-a8f6-7f02-b5c7-9c16b85909d0-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl get service user-service -n social-network'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-9bfcfd0b36886833', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 4648, 'output_tokens': 387, 'total_tokens': 5035}), ToolMessage(content='NAME           TYPE        CLUSTER-IP       EXTERNAL-IP   PORT(S)    AGE\nuser-service   ClusterIP   10.107.135.147   <none>        9090/TCP   2m42s\n', id='79dee029-af7f-4ac7-8e96-0bddfb93b1e8', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-9bfcfd0b36886833'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='e5a29b04-a542-4378-b322-959de1f0d226'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user-service pod is running, and the service is exposed as ClusterIP with port 9090. But the metrics query for up{job="user-service"} returned no results. Hmm. Maybe the service isn\'t actually exposing the metrics endpoint. Wait, the service is ClusterIP, which is only accessible within the cluster. But the metrics query might be expecting an external endpoint. Or maybe the metrics are not being scraped correctly.\n\nWait, the user-service might be using a different job name in Prometheus. The query was up{job="user-service"}, but maybe the actual job name is different. Let me check. Alternatively, maybe the service isn\'t configured to expose the metrics port. Let me check the service\'s YAML to see the target port and port mappings. Also, maybe the pods aren\'t exposing the metrics on the correct port. Or perhaps the Prometheus is not configured to scrape the user-service\'s metrics. \n\nWait, the user-service is a pod, so maybe the service is correctly set up, but the metrics endpoint isn\'t accessible. Let me check the pod\'s container ports. If the user-service is running a metrics server on a different port, like 9091, but the service is mapped to 9090, then Prometheus might not be scraping the correct port. Alternatively, maybe the metrics are not being exposed at all. \n\nAnother angle: the user-service might not be configured to expose metrics. For example, if it\'s a Thrift service, maybe it\'s not set up to serve metrics. Or maybe the metrics are being collected via a different method. \n\nWait, the initial get_metrics query for up{job="user-service"} returned empty. So Prometheus isn\'t finding any targets for that job. Maybe the service isn\'t properly set up as a target. Let me check the service\'s YAML to see if it\'s correctly configured. Also, check if there\'s a Prometheus config that\'s supposed to scrape this service. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the user-service isn\'t running the metrics server. For example, if it\'s a custom service, maybe it\'s not exposing metrics on the expected port. Let me check the pod\'s container ports. \n\nSo, the next step is to get the service\'s YAML to see the port mappings and selectors. Also, check the pod\'s container ports to see if the metrics port is exposed. Maybe the service is pointing to the wrong port. \n\nWait, the service user-service has a port 9090/TCP. But if the pod\'s container is exposing metrics on a different port, like 9091, then the service\'s target port would be wrong. So, I need to check the pod\'s YAML to see the container ports. \n\nSo, the next tool call should be to get the service\'s YAML using kubectl describe service user-service -n social-network. That would show the target port and other configurations. Then, check the pod\'s YAML to see the container ports. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the service is correctly configured, but the metrics are not being scraped. So, checking the Prometheus configuration for the user-service job would be necessary. But since I can\'t access Prometheus directly, maybe the get_metrics tool can be used with a different query. \n\nWait, the user-service is a pod, so maybe the metrics are being collected via a different job name. Let me try a different query, like up{job="user-service-pod"} or something similar. But without knowing the exact job name, it\'s hard to say. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the service is not exposed correctly. If the service is ClusterIP, then it\'s only accessible within the cluster. But if the metrics are being scraped by Prometheus, which is probably running inside the cluster, then that\'s okay. But if the service is not correctly set up, maybe the pods aren\'t being scraped. \n\nSo, the next step is to check the service\'s YAML to see the target port and selectors. Then, check the pod\'s YAML to see if the container is exposing the metrics on the correct port. If the service is pointing to the wrong port, that would explain why the metrics aren\'t being scraped. \n\nTherefore, I should use kubectl describe service user-service -n social-network to get more details about the service configuration.\n</think>\n\nexec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \n"kubectl describe service user-service -n social-network"  \nTo investigate why the user-service metrics are not being scraped by Prometheus, I need to examine the service\'s configuration. This will reveal the target port, selector, and other details that might explain why the service isn\'t being detected. If the service is misconfigured (e.g., incorrect port mapping or selector), it could prevent Prometheus from scraping metrics.', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 976, 'prompt_tokens': 3895, 'total_tokens': 4871, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfd32-bdcb-7781-a2ba-6d36097d7fe9-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 3895, 'output_tokens': 976, 'total_tokens': 4871}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='11c54cc0-2301-4754-b99e-971775375fb7'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let's see. The user-service is running as a pod and has a service entry with ClusterIP, but the metrics query returned no results. The previous step was to get the service details, which showed the service is set up with ClusterIP and port 9090. Now, to understand why Prometheus isn't scraping the metrics, I need to check the service's configuration in more detail.\n\nThe next logical step is to describe the service. Using kubectl describe service user-service -n social-network will give more information about the service, such as the target port, selector, endpoints, and any annotations. This will help verify if the service is correctly pointing to the pod's port and if there are any issues with the endpoints. If the endpoints are not listed, it might indicate a problem with the pod's readiness or the service's selector. Also, checking annotations related to Prometheus scraping could be important. \n\nBy examining the service's describe output, I can confirm if the service is properly configured to expose metrics and if there are any misconfigurations that prevent Prometheus from accessing the metrics endpoint. This information is crucial to pinpoint the root cause of the missing metrics.\n</think>\n\n", additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl describe service user-service -n social-network"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-a9b38c5e74513a9e', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 274, 'prompt_tokens': 4889, 'total_tokens': 5163, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 