ight into why the MongoDB service isn't accepting connections. But first, confirm the pod's status.\n</think>\n\n", additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl get pods -n social-network | grep user-mongodb"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-974a1848e5451f33', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 223, 'prompt_tokens': 8468, 'total_tokens': 8691, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfd33-a45b-7e61-b073-10e5e718427b-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl get pods -n social-network | grep user-mongodb'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-974a1848e5451f33', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 8468, 'output_tokens': 223, 'total_tokens': 8691}), ToolMessage(content='Command Rejected (ValueError): Pipe commands are forbidden', id='f0594093-61c3-4616-aadb-1f55dc25417c', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-974a1848e5451f33'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='6a419ac3-58a1-4e1e-84bd-bde7c14ce048'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user tried to run a command that uses a pipe, but the system rejected it. The available tools don\'t include a way to chain commands with pipes, so I need to adjust.\n\nThe goal is to check the status of the user-mongodb pod. Previously, the user tried to use "kubectl get pods -n social-network | grep user-mongodb", but that\'s not allowed. So, I should use a different approach.\n\nMaybe use "kubectl get pods -n social-network" and then filter the output manually. But since the tool doesn\'t support pipes, I can\'t use grep directly. Alternatively, I can use "kubectl get pods -n social-network" and look for the pod name in the output. \n\nWait, the user might have a pod named user-mongodb. Let me check the previous outputs. Earlier, when they ran "kubectl get pods -n social-network", there was a pod named user-mongodb-6c75c9d7db-lsrds. So the pod name includes the deployment name and a random suffix. \n\nBut the user wants to check if the user-mongodb pod is running. The correct approach is to use "kubectl get pods -n social-network" and look for the pod that matches the user-mongodb deployment. Alternatively, use "kubectl get pods -n social-network --selector=app=user-mongodb" to filter by the app label. \n\nWait, the service for user-mongodb has a selector. Let me check the service details. Earlier, when they described the user-mongodb service, the selector was probably app=user-mongodb. So using the selector with kubectl get pods would list the pods associated with that service. \n\nSo the correct command would be "kubectl get pods -n social-network --selector=app=user-mongodb". That should list the pods managed by the user-mongodb service. If there are no pods or if the pods are not in a Running state, that would explain the connection issues. \n\nTherefore, the next step is to run that command to check the pods associated with user-mongodb. This will confirm if the MongoDB pods are up and running, which is essential for resolving the connection problem.\n</think>\n\nexec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \n"kubectl get pods -n social-network --selector=app=user-mongodb"  \nTo verify the status of the `user-mongodb` pods directly, I need to filter pods by the `app=user-mongodb` label (commonly used as a selector in services). This will confirm if the MongoDB pods are running, have crashes, or are misconfigured, which would explain the connection failures observed in the user-service logs. This avoids relying on grep/pipes and uses Kubernetes\' native selector functionality.', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 564, 'prompt_tokens': 7669, 'total_tokens': 8233, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfd33-b0e3-7012-aa7e-dd0ec1b42031-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 7669, 'output_tokens': 564, 'total_tokens': 8233}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='ed6d66ce-1fdb-4ee7-be2a-577652590c01'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to diagnose why the user-service can\'t connect to user-mongodb. The logs show connection refused errors. So far, they checked the service and endpoints for user-mongodb, which exist and point to a pod. But when they tried to check the pod status with a grep, it didn\'t work. Now they\'re using a selector to get pods with app=user-mongodb.\n\nThe next step is to make sure that the pod is actually running. The previous command using the selector should list the MongoDB pods. If the pod is running, it\'ll show up as "Running". If not, there might be issues like crashes or deployment problems. Also, checking the pod\'s details might reveal more, like if there are restarts or errors. But since the user can only use the available tools, they should first confirm the pod\'s status with the selector. If the pod is running, maybe check the logs next. But since the user-service logs are already showing connection issues, maybe the MongoDB pod is running but not accepting connections. Alternatively, there could be a network policy blocking the connection. But with the tools available, the next logical step is to verify the pod\'s status using the selector. If the pod is up, then check the service configuration again or look into network policies. But given the tools, the next call should be to get the pods with the app label.\n</think>\n\n', additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl get pods -n social-network --selector=app=user-mongodb"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-86693d60b46e066c', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 328, 'prompt_tokens': 8682, 'total_tokens': 9010, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfd33-cc23-7b63-a061-3af44e091834-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl get pods -n social-network --selector=app=user-mongodb'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-86693d60b46e066c', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 8682, 'output_tokens': 328, 'total_tokens': 9010}), ToolMessage(content='NAME                            READY   STATUS    RESTARTS   AGE\nuser-mongodb-6c75c9d7db-lsrds   1/1     Running   0          3m54s\n', id='bef58292-40a0-47c5-9970-8b90d9273ace', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-86693d60b46e066c'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='c50f155e-d648-49a9-994d-8cf34aabf862'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user-service is having trouble connecting to user-mongodb. The logs show connection refused errors. We checked the service and endpoints for user-mongodb, and they seem okay. The pod for user-mongodb is running. Hmm.\n\nWait, maybe the issue is with the network policy or the ports. The service is set to port 27017, and the endpoint shows the pod\'s IP on that port. But maybe there\'s a firewall rule blocking the connection. Or perhaps the MongoDB pod isn\'t actually listening on that port. Let me check the pod\'s configuration.\n\nWait, the user-service is trying to connect to user-mongodb:27017. The service\'s endpoints are pointing to the MongoDB pod\'s IP on 27017. But maybe the MongoDB container isn\'t exposing that port. Let me check the pod\'s container ports. Oh, right, the user-service is using a service account, but maybe there\'s a misconfiguration in the service\'s YAML. Or maybe the MongoDB pod\'s container isn\'t set up to listen on 27017. Wait, the logs from user-service show it\'s trying to connect to user-mongodb:27017, which is the correct port for MongoDB. \n\nWait, but the user-service is a microservice in the social network app. Maybe there\'s a typo in the service name or the port. Let me check the service configuration again. The user-mongodb service has a port 27017, and the endpoint is correct. The pod is running. So why is the connection refused?\n\nAnother angle: maybe the MongoDB pod isn\'t actually running the MongoDB server. Wait, the pod is in Running state, but maybe the container isn\'t healthy. Let me check the logs of the user-mongodb pod to see if the MongoDB service is starting properly. If the MongoDB container is crashing or not starting, that would explain the connection issues. \n\nSo the next step is to get the logs of the user-mongodb pod to check if there are any errors during startup. If the MongoDB server isn\'t starting, that would be the root cause. Let me do that.\n</think>\n\nexec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \n"kubectl logs user-mongodb-6c75c9d7db-lsrds -n social-network"  \nThe `user-mongodb` pod is running, but the connection failures persist. To diagnose why the MongoDB instance isn\'t accepting connections, I need to inspect its logs. This will reveal if the MongoDB server is crashing, failing to start, or encountering network issues (e.g., misconfigured bind IP, port conflicts). This is critical to determine if the service is operational or if there\'s an internal error preventing it from listening on 27017.', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 579, 'prompt_tokens': 7919, 'total_tokens': 8498, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfd33-df22-7f30-a017-f5985043a63f-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 7919, 'output_tokens': 579, 'total_tokens': 8498}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='961d3ab1-bda8-4c97-9cdf-b52908127b0c'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user-service is having trouble connecting to user-mongodb. The logs show connection refused errors. I checked the service and endpoints for user-mongodb, and they seem correctly set up. The pod is running, so the next step is to look into the MongoDB logs to find out why it\'s not accepting connections.\n\nI need to use kubectl to get the logs of the user-mongodb pod. The command would be "kubectl logs user-mongodb-6c75c9d7db-lsrds -n social-network". This will show any errors or issues the MongoDB instance is encountering, like configuration problems, port conflicts, or crashes. If the logs indicate a problem, that\'s the root cause. If not, maybe check the network policies or firewall settings, but the logs are the first place to look.\n</think>\n\n', additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl logs user-mongodb-6c75c9d7db-lsrds -n social-network"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-bac43967da380523', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'toke