the logs command. \n\nBut since the user can only make one tool call at a time, they need to first retrieve the pod name. So the next step is to execute kubectl get pods -n social-network to find the user-service pod's name. Once they have that, they can then get the logs. Alternatively, if the deployment is named user-service, they can use kubectl logs for the specific pod. \n\nWait, the user might not have the pod name, so the first step is to get the list of pods. Let me structure the tool call accordingly. The correct command would be kubectl get pods -n social-network to list all pods in that namespace. Then, from the output, they can identify the user-service pod's name. After that, they can run kubectl logs on that specific pod. \n\nSo the next tool call should be to get the pods in the social-network namespace. That way, the user can find the exact pod name to use in the logs command. This makes sense because without the pod name, the logs command can't be executed properly. Therefore, the next step is to retrieve the list of pods.\n</think>\n\n", additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl get pods -n social-network"}', 'name': 'exec_kubectl_cmd_safely'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-b29cda2139af1008', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 610, 'prompt_tokens': 4155, 'total_tokens': 4765, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfd35-6848-7600-99b6-bad76ad92937-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_kubectl_cmd_safely', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl get pods -n social-network'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-b29cda2139af1008', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 4155, 'output_tokens': 610, 'total_tokens': 4765}), ToolMessage(content='NAME