al whether the issue is due to misconfiguration, network restrictions, or application-level errors. \n\ntool_call: {"command": "kubectl logs -n social-network user-service-559b79c875-gcl2q"}', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 393, 'prompt_tokens': 4210, 'total_tokens': 4603, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfd35-8764-7280-8134-9a855b0c8530-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 4210, 'output_tokens': 393, 'total_tokens': 4603}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='6af1a706-3e5b-4321-bf2a-27d9ed5bbc81'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let me try to figure out what\'s going on here. The user is dealing with a social network application where the user-service can\'t connect to the user-mongodb database. Both the service and the pod are running, and the MongoDB is listening on the correct port. But when the user-service tries to connect, it gets a "connection refused" error.\n\nFirst, I checked the pods and deployments to make sure everything was up. The user-service pod is running, and the user-mongodb service has endpoints pointing to the correct pod. The MongoDB logs show it\'s listening on 0.0.0.0:27017, so that\'s not the issue. \n\nThe next step was to check the network policies. There were no NetworkPolicies in the namespace, so Kubernetes shouldn\'t be blocking the traffic. But the connection is still refused. That makes me think there might be a firewall rule or a security group blocking the traffic between the pods. \n\nI tried using the get_dependency_graph tool to see if there were any issues in the service dependencies, but it returned no data. That might mean there\'s no dependency graph data for the last 5 minutes, or maybe the tool isn\'t capturing that information. \n\nThen, I checked the logs of the user-service pod. The logs show a "connection refused" error when trying to connect to user-mongodb:27017. The IP address in the error is 10.244.1.230, which is the IP of the user-mongodb pod. But the connection is still being blocked. \n\nSince the pods are in the same namespace and there\'s no NetworkPolicy, the issue must be at the network layer. Maybe the host\'s firewall or the cloud provider\'s security groups are blocking the traffic on port 27017. I should check if there are any firewall rules that are preventing communication between the pods. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the MongoDB pod isn\'t properly exposed as a service. Wait, the user-mongodb service is running and has endpoints, so that\'s not the problem. The service is correctly pointing to the pod\'s IP and port. \n\nAnother possibility is that the user-service is using the wrong DNS name or service name to connect to MongoDB. But the service name is user-mongodb, which should resolve correctly within the cluster. \n\nGiven that all the configurations seem correct, the most likely cause is a network policy or firewall rule blocking the traffic. The user needs to check the host\'s firewall settings and any cloud provider security groups to ensure port 27017 is open between the user-service and user-mongodb pods. \n\nSo, the final step is to inform the user that the root cause is a network policy or firewall rule blocking the connection, and they should check those settings to allow traffic on port 27017 between the pods.\n</think>\n\n', additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl logs -n social-network user-service-559b79c875-gcl2q"}', 'name': 'exec_kubectl_cmd_safely'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-98d7925ae30f2265', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata=